.TH ONIONFRUITUX 1 "October 2026" "OnionFruitux 1.0.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
onionfruitux \- send this computer's internet through Tor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B onionfruitux
.br
.B onionfruitux
.I tray
.br
.B onionfruitux route add
.I name
[\fB\-\-entry\fR \fIcc\fR]
[\fB\-\-exit\fR \fIcc\fR]
[\fB\-\-bridge\fR \fIkind\fR]
[\fB\-\-strict\fR]
.br
.B onionfruitux route list
.br
.B onionfruitux route use
.I name
.br
.B onionfruitux route remove
.I name
.br
.B onionfruitux connect
.br
.B onionfruitux status
.br
.B onionfruitux new\-circuit
.br
.B onionfruitux disconnect
.br
.B onionfruitux panic\-off
.br
.B onionfruitux doctor
.br
.B onionfruitux plan
.br
.B onionfruitux boot enable
.br
.B onionfruitux boot disable
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B OnionFruitux
is a Linux switch that sends the computer's new internet connections through
the Tor network. Open the window and turn the switch on, or run
.BR onionfruitux\ connect .
Turn the switch off and the computer uses its normal network again.
.PP
While the switch is on, OnionFruitux runs Tor as the system user
.B onionfruitux
and adds one nftables table named
.BR onionfruitux .
TCP connections are redirected into Tor. DNS is redirected into Tor, including
lookups that would have gone to a home router, and
.B .onion
names can resolve through Tor's DNS port. DHCP keeps working. Other UDP is
blocked. The local network stays direct unless that setting is turned off.
Traffic that cannot go through Tor is rejected unless that check is turned off.
.PP
A redirected packet is still on its original network interface when the filter
hook runs, so the loopback accept does not match it. The filter accepts
packets with the DNAT conntrack status, which is how the redirect is marked,
and those packets are delivered to Tor. Tor's mapped addresses,
10.192.0.0/10 and fc00:192::/32, are redirected as well. They are not treated
as the local network.
.PP
On Ubuntu, programs ask systemd\-resolved at 127.0.0.53. While the switch is
on, OnionFruitux tells resolved to forward to 127.0.0.1 port 9153 and does not
replace the stub\-resolv.conf symlink. Turning the switch off, or running
.BR panic\-off ,
puts the previous resolver back. A machine without systemd\-resolved gets a
temporary
.I nameserver 127.0.0.1
line, and the original file is restored afterward. Port 53 is still redirected,
so a lookup aimed at the home router enters Tor either way.
.PP
Tailscale's encrypted UDP does not pass the UDP block, so the Tailscale tunnel
pauses while the switch is on and returns when the switch is turned off.
MagicDNS is not used during that time. Web and other TCP still enter Tor,
including TCP that would have gone out through Tailscale, so that path is not
a way around Tor. Printers and other machines on the local network stay
reachable, except for DNS.
.PP
Turning the switch off deletes only the
.B onionfruitux
table and stops the Tor process OnionFruitux started.
.PP
The window stays responsive while the switch is turning on. The switch slides
on and turns orange, and a status line reports each step, including Tor's
bootstrap percent. It turns green only when Tor is fully connected and the
firewall table is installed. The same orange and green states are used on the
tray icon. If connecting fails or takes too long, the switch returns to off,
the table is removed, and Tor is stopped. Closing or force\-quitting the app
during connect does the same rollback.
.PP
The firewall is installed only after Tor's log reports Bootstrapped 100%.
Until then the computer keeps its normal network.
.PP
OnionFruitux runs its own Tor. The transparent proxy is port 9140, DNS is
port 9153, SOCKS is port 9155, and the control port is 9156. Those ports
are not 9050 or 9051, which the
.B tor
package uses, and not 5353, which mDNS uses. Installing this package does
not stop or disable Ubuntu's Tor service. If that service is running,
.B onionfruitux doctor
says so and leaves it running. To stop it yourself:
.EX
sudo systemctl disable \-\-now tor.service tor@default.service
.EE
If one of OnionFruitux's ports is already taken, the switch names the
process that holds it. If Tor itself exits, the error includes the last
lines of its log.
.PP
After the switch turns fully on, OnionFruitux opens
.I https://check.torproject.org/
in the desktop user's browser. The page opens only after that successful
connection. It is not opened by the root process that changes the firewall,
and the program does not wait for the browser. Turning the switch off opens
the page again after the table is removed. Turn this off under Settings,
Network, with
.B "Open the Tor check page when the switch changes" .
The boot service does not open a browser.
.PP
The first time the switch is turned on, the desktop asks for an administrator
password so OnionFruitux can change the firewall. Polkit remembers that
password for a little while. The window is not frozen while that dialog is open.
.PP
OnionFruitux is an independent project by Nitrofire Computing. It is not
affiliated with or endorsed by The Tor Project or DragonFruit Network.
.SH COMMANDS
With no arguments,
.B onionfruitux
opens the window. The window has the switch,
.BR "New circuit" ,
and
.BR Settings .
.TP
.B tray
Show a panel or dock icon with the same switch. The menu can turn the switch
on or off, ask for a new circuit, or open the window.
.TP
.B route add \fIname\fR
Save a named route. A later
.B route add
with the same name replaces that route.
.B \-\-entry
and
.B \-\-exit
take a two\-letter country code.
.B \-\-bridge
is one of
.BR plain ,
.BR obfs4 ,
.BR snowflake ,
.BR meek ,
.BR webtunnel ,
or
.BR conjure .
A bridge replaces the entry country.
.B \-\-strict
means only use the countries on the route.
.TP
.B route list
List saved routes. The active route is marked with an asterisk.
.TP
.B route use \fIname\fR
Choose the route the next connection will use. If the switch is already on,
turn it off and on to use the new route.
.TP
.B route remove \fIname\fR
Delete a saved route.
.TP
.B connect
Turn the switch on. This starts Tor, waits until Tor is fully connected, then
installs the firewall table. On failure the table is removed.
.TP
.B disconnect
Turn the switch off.
.TP
.B panic\-off
Remove the
.B onionfruitux
firewall table and stop OnionFruitux's Tor immediately, even if the window
froze or the saved state says the switch is already off. This does not open a
browser. It is safe to run twice. It removes the firewall table, points DNS
back at the previous resolver, and stops OnionFruitux's Tor.
.TP
.B status
Print whether the switch is on, and the active route.
.TP
.B new\-circuit
Ask Tor for a fresh path. The switch must already be on.
.TP
.B doctor
Check that Tor, nftables, the
.B onionfruitux
user, GeoIP data, and any selected bridge transport are present. When
.B nft
can run, the planned rules are checked with
.B nft \-c
and are not installed.
.B doctor
does not change the network or start Tor.
.TP
.B plan
Print the torrc and the nftables rules for the current settings. This command
changes nothing.
.TP
.B boot enable
Save the current settings and turn routing on at startup. It does not turn the
switch on now.
.TP
.B boot disable
Do not turn routing on at startup. It does not turn off a switch that is
already on.
.SH SETTINGS
Open
.B Settings
in the window.
.TP
.B Routes
.B New route
asks for a name, an entry country, and an exit country. Sites see the exit
country. Choose
.B "Only use the countries I picked"
when a circuit that ignores those countries is not acceptable. A route can
also remember a bridge.
.TP
.B Bridges
Optional. Plain, obfs4, Snowflake, meek, WebTunnel, and Conjure. Snowflake,
meek, and obfs4 can use the published default bridges shipped in
.IR onionfruitux/data/pt_config.json .
Paste lines from
.I https://bridges.torproject.org
when a network blocks the defaults, or for plain, WebTunnel, and Conjure.
.TP
.B Network
Keep the local network off Tor, block traffic that cannot use Tor, and list
relay ports (for example
.IR "80, 443" )
when a firewall only allows those outbound ports. Those ports are written as
Tor's
.B FascistFirewall
and
.B ReachableAddresses
settings.
.SH INSTALL
Debian, Ubuntu, Linux Mint, Pop!_OS, Fedora, Arch, Manjaro, and openSUSE:

.EX
git clone https://github.com/nitrofireinc-pixel/OnionFruitux.git
cd OnionFruitux
sudo ./install.sh
.EE

The installer uses apt, dnf, pacman, or zypper. It installs Tor, nftables, a
bridge transport when the distribution has one (lyrebird, obfs4proxy, or
snowflake\-client), and the Qt libraries used by the window. It creates the
system user
.BR onionfruitux .
It installs this manual to
.I /usr/share/man/man1/onionfruitux.1.gz
and refreshes the manual index with
.B mandb
when that program is present.
.PP
Then pin OnionFruitux to the dock or panel, or run
.B onionfruitux
or
.BR "onionfruitux tray" .
.SH UNINSTALL
.EX
sudo ./uninstall.sh
.EE
removes the program, this manual, the firewall table, and OnionFruitux's Tor
process. The
.B tor
and
.B nftables
packages stay installed.
.EX
sudo ./uninstall.sh \-\-purge
.EE
also removes the
.B onionfruitux
user and
.IR /var/lib/onionfruitux .
.SH RECOVERY
If the computer has no working internet after the app was closed, check for
the firewall table and remove it. These commands work even on 1.0.0, which
does not have
.BR panic\-off :
.EX
sudo nft list table inet onionfruitux
sudo nft delete table inet onionfruitux
sudo /usr/bin/onionfruitux \-\-system disconnect
.EE
.B nft delete
is enough to restore DNS and normal routing.
.B \-\-system disconnect
also stops OnionFruitux's Tor and does not open a browser. Then, if a Tor
process was started with OnionFruitux's torrc and is still running, stop that
process only:
.EX
ps \-eo pid,args | grep /var/lib/onionfruitux/torrc
sudo kill PID
.EE
On 1.0.1 or later, one command does both:
.EX
onionfruitux panic\-off
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/onionfruitux/config.json
Routes, bridge lines, and network settings for the current user.
.I XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is used when it is set.
.TP
.I /var/lib/onionfruitux/torrc
The torrc written when the switch turns on.
.TP
.I /var/lib/onionfruitux/tor/
Tor's data directory, owned by the
.B onionfruitux
user.
.TP
.I /var/lib/onionfruitux/state.json
Whether the switch is on. Status reads this file.
.TP
.I /etc/onionfruitux/boot.json
Settings saved by
.BR "boot enable" .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/onionfruitux.service
Startup unit. It is installed disabled.
.B boot enable
enables it and does not start it.
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/onionfruitux.1.gz
This manual.
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit\-1/actions/com.nitrofire.onionfruitux.policy
Administrator password prompt for the firewall change.
.TP
.I /usr/share/tor/geoip
Country database from the
.B tor
package. Country selection needs it.
.TP
.I onionfruitux/data/pt_config.json
Published default bridges.
.I onionfruitux/data/PT_CONFIG_SOURCE.txt
records the Tor Project file they were copied from.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tor (1),
.BR nft (8)
